course: dictionary
lesson: 3780
title: Foolish: silly or not sensible
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Foolish means silly or not sensible. It shows poor judgement, which means someone did not think carefully before they acted.
explain: «It would be foolish to swim alone in the sea.» Nobody should do this, because if you got into trouble, no one would be there to help. The clues in the sentence, the sea and swimming alone, show you that foolish means risky and not sensible.
explain: Foolish is a softer word than stupid. To feel foolish is to feel silly or embarrassed, and to look foolish is to seem stupid to other people. Always check the sentence: does your meaning still make sense?
fact: Foolish is an adjective, and you can say more foolish or most foolish. Its adverb is foolishly.
fact: «Foolish» means silly or not sensible, and it shows poor judgement.
fact: «Feel foolish» means to feel silly or embarrassed.
fact: In stories and fables, foolish characters often make bad choices and learn a lesson.
fact: «Under emperors, cruel or foolish rulers like Caligula and Nero did much harm.»
exercise: «It would be foolish to swim alone in the sea.» What does foolish mean here? | Brave and bold | Fast and strong | Very cold | *Not sensible, so likely to get into danger
exercise: Which phrase means to feel silly or embarrassed? | to feel tall | *to feel foolish | to feel brave | to feel hungry
exercise: Which word is the adverb made from foolish? | fool's | *foolishly | foolisher | foolish
test: Compared with stupid, what is special about foolish? | A word meaning very big | A word for animals | A much stronger word | *A softer word
test: What does «to look foolish» mean? | To be very tall | To run very fast | To sing loudly | *To seem stupid to other people
exam: In the phrase «a foolish mistake», what does foolish mean? | A mistake made on purpose by a clever person | *A silly mistake that was not sensible | A mistake about numbers | A mistake that is very long
